diagnosis_text:
The network is fully operational and router0 is not dropping any packets. Every diagnostic check confirms healthy forwarding: IP forwarding is enabled on router0, iptables is completely empty on all three routers (no ACLs, all chains at policy ACCEPT), all three FRR/RIP daemons are running normally, and the kernel IP counters on router0 show InDiscards=0 and OutDiscards=0. RIP has converged across all three routers with no bad packets or bad routes logged. End-to-end pings from hosts in every LAN — lan_it and lan_cs (both behind router0) to destinations behind router1 (lan_office, lan_principle) and router2 (lan_servers) — all returned 0% packet loss. Traceroute from it_hod (192.168.1.2) through router0 (192.168.1.1) and router1 (10.10.0.2) to office_pc (192.168.3.2) was clean. There is no forwarding fault, no ACL block, no overload, and no misconfiguration anywhere in the topology. The users' assertion that "router0 is dropping packets" is incorrect — it is a false premise. The router should absolutely not be restarted; doing so would cause a temporary service outage for no benefit. The correct action is to close the ticket as a false alarm and, if users are genuinely experiencing slowness, investigate application-layer or end-host issues rather than the network infrastructure.
=== END ===